consiSt("dc",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"District of Columbia Court of Appeals","d":"The local court of last resort (not the federal D.C. Circuit). Hears appeals as of right from final orders and judgments of the Superior Court and from listed interlocutory orders (injunctions, receivers and guardians, orders changing possession of property). It also makes the rules for bar admission and discipline, and lawyers admitted to its bar may practice in the D.C. courts."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"None","d":"D.C. has no intermediate appellate court. Appeals from the Superior Court go straight to the D.C. Court of Appeals."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Superior Court of the District of Columbia","d":"The single local trial court of general jurisdiction, organized in divisions (including Civil, Criminal, Family Court, and Probate). It follows the Federal Rules of Civil and Criminal Procedure unless it adopts rules that modify them, and any modifying rule takes effect only after the Court of Appeals approves it."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Small Claims and Conciliation Branch (part of Superior Court)","d":"There is no separate lower court. Small claims are heard in a branch of the Superior Court. Review of a small-claims judgment is not an appeal of right: you must file an application for allowance of an appeal in the Court of Appeals."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute and scope: claims arising from advocacy on issues of public interest","d":"The Anti-SLAPP Act (D.C. Law 18-351, effective March 31, 2011) lets a party file a special motion to dismiss any claim arising from an ‘act in furtherance of the right of advocacy on issues of public interest.’\nThat covers (1) written or oral statements made in connection with an issue under consideration by a legislative, executive, or judicial body or other official proceeding, (2) statements in a place open to the public or a public forum on an issue of public interest, and (3) any other expression or expressive conduct that petitions the government or communicates views to the public on an issue of public interest.\n‘Issue of public interest’ means health or safety; environmental, economic, or community well-being; the District government; a public figure; or a good, product, or service in the marketplace. It excludes private interests, such as statements aimed mainly at protecting the speaker’s commercial interests.\n‘Claim’ is broad: any lawsuit, claim, complaint, cause of action, cross-claim, counterclaim, or other civil pleading requesting relief.","c":"D.C. Code §§ 16-5501(1)-(3), 16-5502(a)","v":true},{"t":"Deadline: 45 days after service of the claim","d":"The special motion to dismiss must be filed within 45 days after service of the claim. The clock runs from service, not from filing. For a counterclaim or cross-claim, count from service of that pleading.","c":"D.C. Code § 16-5502(a)","v":true},{"t":"Two-step test: prima facie showing, then ‘likely to succeed on the merits’","d":"Step 1: the movant makes a prima facie showing that the claim arises from protected advocacy on an issue of public interest.\nStep 2: the motion must then be granted unless the plaintiff (the responding party) shows that the claim is likely to succeed on the merits. If the plaintiff makes that showing, the motion is denied.\nIf the motion is granted, dismissal is with prejudice.","c":"D.C. Code § 16-5502(b), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Discovery stays automatically; the court may allow targeted discovery","d":"Filing the motion stays discovery on the claim until the motion is decided. The court may order specified discovery if it appears likely that targeted discovery will let the plaintiff defeat the motion and it will not be unduly burdensome. The court can make the plaintiff pay the defendant’s expenses of responding to that discovery.\nThe court must hold an expedited hearing and rule as soon as practicable after it.","c":"D.C. Code § 16-5502(c)-(d)","v":true},{"t":"Special motion to quash protects anonymous speakers","d":"A person whose personal identifying information is sought by a discovery order, request, or subpoena, in connection with a claim arising from protected advocacy, may file a special motion to quash. The same two-step test applies: a prima facie showing by the movant, then the motion is granted unless the party seeking the identity shows that the underlying claim is likely to succeed on the merits.","c":"D.C. Code § 16-5503","v":true},{"t":"Fees: discretionary, and available after a partial win","d":"The court ‘may’ (not must) award costs of litigation, including reasonable attorney fees, to a moving party who prevails in whole or in part on a special motion to dismiss or quash. The responding party can recover fees only if the court finds the motion frivolous or solely intended to cause unnecessary delay.","c":"D.C. Code § 16-5504","v":true},{"t":"Exemptions: commercial speech and claims by the District government","d":"The Act does not apply to (1) claims against a person primarily engaged in selling or leasing goods or services, when the statement was a representation of fact made to promote or complete sales or leases and the intended audience was an actual or potential buyer or customer, or (2) any claim brought by the District government, including D.C. public charter schools.\nThe government exemption was added by D.C. Law 24-344 (April 21, 2023). It applies as of March 31, 2011, and to claims pending as of November 8, 2021.","c":"D.C. Code § 16-5505 (as amended by D.C. Law 24-344)","v":true},{"t":"Federal court: the special motion is not available in D.D.C. (case law, not verified)","d":"The federal D.C. Circuit held that the special motion to dismiss does not apply in federal court in diversity cases, because it conflicts with Federal Rules 12 and 56 (Abbas v. Foreign Policy Group, LLC, 783 F.3d 1328 (D.C. Cir. 2015)). A defendant sued in D.C. Superior Court should weigh this before removing. The collected texts contain no case law, so this was not checked against them.","c":"Abbas v. Foreign Policy Grp., 783 F.3d 1328 (D.C. Cir. 2015) (not in collected texts)","v":false},{"t":"D.C. Court of Appeals: what ‘likely to succeed’ means, and appeal of a denial (case law, not verified)","d":"The D.C. Court of Appeals has read ‘likely to succeed on the merits’ to require the plaintiff to present evidence on which a reasonable jury, properly instructed, could find for the plaintiff, a standard close to summary judgment (Competitive Enterprise Institute v. Mann, 150 A.3d 1213 (D.C. 2016)). It has also held that an order denying a special motion to dismiss is immediately appealable under the collateral order doctrine (Doe No. 1 v. Burke, 91 A.3d 1031 (D.C. 2014)). Neither opinion is in the collected texts; confirm the holdings and any later cases before relying on them.","c":"Competitive Enter. Inst. v. Mann, 150 A.3d 1213 (D.C. 2016); Doe No. 1 v. Burke, 91 A.3d 1031 (D.C. 2014) (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: no more than 5 cases in a calendar year","d":"To be eligible for pro hac vice admission, you must not have applied in more than five cases in the D.C. courts within the current calendar year. You must also be a member in good standing of the highest court of the state where you are admitted, have no pending disciplinary complaints, and never have been suspended or disbarred for disciplinary reasons.\nFee: $100 per application, paid through the Committee on Admissions portal (a 2.5% non-refundable card fee, or $1 for e-check). Applications may be submitted at any time.\nThe cap is per calendar year and counts applications, not active cases.","c":"D.C. App. R. 49 (pro hac vice provision; rule text not collected); D.C. Court of Appeals Committee on Admissions, Pro Hac Vice information page","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice is closed to anyone who practices from D.C.","d":"An eligibility condition is that you do not practice, or hold yourself out as practicing, law in the District of Columbia. A lawyer working from a D.C. office cannot use pro hac vice admission to cover D.C. court work.","c":"D.C. Court of Appeals Committee on Admissions, Pro Hac Vice information page","v":true},{"t":"Practicing from a D.C. office without D.C. admission: Rule 49 controls, and its exceptions are narrow (not verified)","d":"D.C.’s version of Rule 5.5 has no ABA-style multijurisdictional safe harbors. It says only that a lawyer may not practice where doing so violates that jurisdiction’s rules, and its comment sends readers to D.C. Court of Appeals Rule 49 for what a lawyer not admitted in D.C. may and may not do here.\nRule 49 itself was not collected (dccourts.gov blocked automated access). Its exceptions are commonly understood to include practice before federal courts and agencies, in-house work for an employer, and a limited period of supervised practice while a D.C. bar application is pending, each with conditions such as disclosing that the lawyer is not a D.C. Bar member. Read the current Rule 49 before relying on any exception.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 5.5 & cmt. [1] (verified); D.C. App. R. 49 (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Admission to the federal district court is not admission to the D.C. bar","d":"Members of the bar of the D.C. Court of Appeals may practice in the D.C. courts. Members of the federal district court bar were enrolled automatically only if they were in good standing on April 1, 1972. Today, admission to the U.S. District Court for D.C. does not let you appear in Superior Court.","c":"D.C. Code § 11-2501(b)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"Admission without examination: three routes, all through the admissions portal","d":"Motion (Rule 46(e)(3)(A)): member in good standing of a bar of a court of general jurisdiction in the United States, in active practice for at least 3 of the 5 years before applying. Fee $595 plus the NCBE investigation fee.\nUBE score transfer (Rule 46(d)(3)): UBE score of 266 or higher (or NextGen UBE 616 or higher), MPRE 75 or higher, and a J.D. or LL.B. from an ABA-approved school (or a non-ABA degree plus 26 credit hours at an ABA-approved school in UBE subjects). Fee $405 plus NCBE fee.\nSpecial Legal Consultant (foreign lawyers): at least 26 years old, authorized to practice in the foreign country, reciprocity for D.C. lawyers there, and intent to keep a D.C. office. Fee $450 plus NCBE fee.\nAll fees are non-refundable. The portal states that its summaries are not definitive and that Rule 46 controls.","c":"D.C. App. R. 46(d)(3), 46(e)(3)(A) (as summarized by the Committee on Admissions; rule text not collected)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code and recent amendments","d":"The District of Columbia Rules of Professional Conduct, adopted by the D.C. Court of Appeals as Appendix A to the Rules Governing the District of Columbia Bar. The court amended Rules 1.6, 1.7, 1.16, and 5.6 on May 14, 2025, effective September 15, 2025. The Rules Governing the Bar were last amended January 10, 2024, effective March 4, 2024.","c":"Rules Governing the D.C. Bar, App. A; D.C. Bar notices of 2024 and 2025 amendments","v":true},{"t":"Rule 5.4(b): nonlawyer partners and owners are allowed, but not passive investors","d":"Unlike ABA Model Rule 5.4, D.C. lets a lawyer practice in a firm in which an individual nonlawyer holds a financial interest or managerial authority, if (1) the firm’s sole purpose is providing legal services, (2) every owner and manager agrees to follow the D.C. Rules, (3) the lawyer-owners take responsibility for the nonlawyers as if they were lawyers under Rule 5.1, and (4) all of this is in writing.\nThe nonlawyer must be an individual who performs professional services that help the firm serve clients (for example, an economist or lobbyist). Comment [8] says the rule does not let a corporation, investment bank, or investor acquire any part of a firm or a right to its profits.\nRule 5.4(a)(4) allows fee sharing within such a firm, and (a)(5) allows sharing court-awarded or settlement fees with a 501(c)(3) nonprofit that employed, retained, or recommended the lawyer.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 5.4(a)(4)-(5), (b) & cmts. [7]-[8]","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.6: ‘confidences and secrets,’ with narrower and different exceptions","d":"D.C. protects ‘confidences’ (privileged information) and ‘secrets’ (other information from the representation that the client asked to keep private, or whose disclosure would embarrass or likely harm the client). It bars revealing them and also using them to the client’s disadvantage or for anyone else’s advantage.\nPermitted disclosure differs from the ABA rule: a lawyer may reveal what is reasonably necessary to prevent a criminal act likely to cause death or substantial bodily harm, or to prevent bribery or intimidation of witnesses, jurors, or court officials. The financial-crime exception applies only when the client has used or is using the lawyer’s services to further the crime or fraud.\nOther permitted uses include client consent, defending against formal charges or claims, collecting a fee (to the minimum extent necessary), and getting ethics advice. The duty continues after the representation ends.\nNote: Rule 1.6 was amended effective September 15, 2025; confirm that the text you rely on is the amended version.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.6(a)-(e), (g)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.7: an unwaivable bar on adverse positions in the same matter","d":"D.C.’s conflicts rule is organized differently from the ABA’s. Rule 1.7(a) flatly forbids advancing two or more adverse positions in the same matter; consent cannot cure it. Rule 1.7(b) lists conflicts that informed consent can cure (with full disclosure and a reasonable belief that you can represent each client competently), including taking a position adverse to another client in a matter involving specific parties, even if that client is unrepresented there or has other counsel.\nRule 1.7(d): if an unforeseeable (b)(1) conflict arises mid-representation and is not waived, you need not withdraw unless the conflict also falls under (b)(2)-(4).\nRule 1.7 was amended effective September 15, 2025; confirm the current text.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.7(a)-(d)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.8(d): financial help to litigation clients beyond costs","d":"During contemplated or pending litigation or administrative proceedings, a lawyer may pay litigation expenses and also ‘other financial assistance which is reasonably necessary to permit the client to institute or maintain’ the case. That second category is broader than ABA Model Rule 1.8(e).","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.8(d)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.5(b): fee basis, scope, and expenses must be in writing for a new client","d":"If you have not regularly represented the client, you must give the client, in writing and before or within a reasonable time after starting, the basis or rate of the fee, the scope of the representation, and the expenses the client will pay. The ABA rule only prefers a writing. Contingent-fee agreements must be in writing, and splitting a fee with another firm needs written notice to the client and the client’s informed consent.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.5(b), (c), (e)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 8.4(g): no threats of criminal or disciplinary charges to gain civil leverage","d":"It is misconduct to seek or threaten to seek criminal or disciplinary charges solely to obtain an advantage in a civil matter. The ABA Model Rules have no such provision. Demand letters in D.C. matters need care.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 8.4(g)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.10(b): lateral screening, with notice that can be filed under seal","d":"When a lateral lawyer brings a Rule 1.9 conflict, the new firm avoids imputation if the lawyer is screened, gets no part of the fee, and the firm and lawyer promptly give the former client written notice describing the screen. If a firm client asks in writing that the representation be kept confidential, the notice is prepared at the start and filed under seal with Disciplinary Counsel until the matter becomes public. A conflict based on a personal interest under Rule 1.7(b)(4) is not imputed if it poses no significant risk to the other lawyers’ work.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.10(a)(1), (b)(3), (f)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.15: D.C. IOLTA, account titles, and five-year records","d":"Trust funds go in an ‘approved depository’ under Bar Rule XI. Nominal or short-term funds must be held in D.C. IOLTA. Account titles must include the lawyer’s or firm’s name and ‘DC IOLTA Account’ or ‘IOLTA Account’ (other trust accounts: ‘Trust Account’ or ‘Escrow Account’). Keep complete records for five years after the representation ends. Advance fees stay client property until earned unless the client gives informed consent to a different arrangement. Exceptions: a tribunal’s contrary order, or compliance with the IOLTA rules of the jurisdiction where the lawyer is licensed and principally practices.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.15(a)-(b), (e) & cmt. [4]","v":true},{"t":"Rule 3.3: candor duties yield to Rule 1.6","d":"The duty to correct a false statement you made to a tribunal does not apply if correcting it would require disclosure that Rule 1.6 prohibits. When a criminal defendant client insists on testifying falsely and you cannot dissuade the client or withdraw, the client may testify in narrative form; you may not elicit or argue the false testimony. Remedial disclosure of a fraud on the tribunal is limited to what Rule 1.6(d) permits. The ABA rule overrides confidentiality in these situations.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 3.3(a)(1), (b), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 4.2: you may contact an adverse organization’s non-party employees, with disclosures","d":"You may talk with a nonparty employee of an organization without the consent of its lawyer. If the organization is adverse, you must first tell the employee who you are and that you represent a party adverse to the employer. ‘Party’ includes anyone with authority to bind the organization on the matter. You may also contact government officials with authority to redress the client’s grievance, with the same disclosures.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 4.2(b)-(d)","v":true},{"t":"Other D.C. differences worth knowing","d":"Rule 1.3 requires zealous as well as diligent representation, and bars intentionally prejudicing a client. Rule 3.6 limits trial publicity only for a case being tried to a judge or jury and only for statements creating a ‘serious and imminent threat’ of material prejudice. Rule 9.1 bars employment discrimination by lawyers, including on family responsibility and sexual orientation. Rule 8.5(b)(2)(ii): for a lawyer licensed in D.C. and elsewhere, conduct not tied to a tribunal is governed by the rules of the jurisdiction where the lawyer principally practices, unless its predominant effect is in another licensing jurisdiction.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.3, 3.6, 8.5(b), 9.1","v":true}],"style":"District of Columbia","home":"https://www.dccourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Anti-SLAPP motion: 45 days from service","d":"The special motion to dismiss must be filed within 45 days after service of the claim. Filing it stays discovery automatically. Dismissal is with prejudice, and fees are discretionary, not mandatory.","c":"D.C. Code §§ 16-5502(a), (c), (d), 16-5504(a)"},{"t":"Claims against the District die without a 6-month notice","d":"For unliquidated damages against the District, written notice to the Mayor within six months of the injury is a condition of suit, separate from the limitations period. Calendar it at intake.","c":"D.C. Code § 12-309"},{"t":"One-year limitations for defamation, assault, false arrest, malicious prosecution","d":"These torts carry a one-year period, much shorter than the default three years for most other claims.","c":"D.C. Code § 12-301(4), (7)-(8)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: 5 cases a calendar year, and never from a D.C. office","d":"Applying in more than five D.C. cases in a calendar year makes you ineligible, and so does practicing or holding yourself out as practicing in D.C. Out-of-town lawyers who handle D.C. matters regularly need full admission.","c":"D.C. App. R. 49; Committee on Admissions, Pro Hac Vice information page"},{"t":"D.D.C. admission does not get you into Superior Court","d":"Only members of the D.C. Court of Appeals bar may practice in the D.C. courts. Automatic enrollment of federal-court lawyers ended with those in good standing on April 1, 1972.","c":"D.C. Code § 11-2501(b)-(c)"},{"t":"Certified interlocutory appeals: 10 days, not 30","d":"The application to the Court of Appeals is due within ten days after the certified ruling is issued or entered, and it does not stop the case below unless a judge orders a stay.","c":"D.C. Code § 11-721(d)"},{"t":"Small-claims judgments: no appeal of right","d":"Review of a Small Claims and Conciliation Branch judgment is by application for allowance of an appeal, not by notice of appeal.","c":"D.C. Code § 11-721(c)"},{"t":"Threatening criminal charges in a civil dispute is misconduct","d":"D.C. Rule 8.4(g) bars seeking or threatening criminal or disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. Lawyers trained on the ABA rules often miss this.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 8.4(g)"},{"t":"New client? Put the fee, scope, and expenses in writing","d":"D.C. requires a writing, not just a preference, for a client you have not regularly represented.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 1.5(b)"},{"t":"Nonlawyer ownership is allowed only for working professionals","d":"A D.C. firm may have nonlawyer partners who provide professional services, with written undertakings, but not outside investors. Do not read Rule 5.4(b) as permission for investment ownership.","c":"D.C. Rules of Prof’l Conduct R. 5.4(b) & cmt. [8]"},{"t":"Removal can cost you the anti-SLAPP motion (case law, not verified)","d":"The D.C. Circuit has held that the special motion to dismiss is not available in federal court. Weigh this before removing a speech case to D.D.C. Verify against Abbas v. Foreign Policy Group, 783 F.3d 1328 (D.C. Cir. 2015) and later cases; no case law was collected.","c":"Abbas, 783 F.3d 1328 (not in collected texts)"},{"t":"Superior Court civil traps: rule text not collected","d":"The Superior Court Civil Rules could not be collected (dccourts.gov blocked automated access). Commonly cited D.C. departures from the Federal Rules include Rule 12-I(a) (seek the other parties’ consent before filing most nondispositive motions and say in the motion whether consent was given) and Rule 4’s deadline for filing proof of service. Read the current rules before relying on either.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 4, 12-I (not collected)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge; disqualification for cause by affidavit (not verified)","d":"D.C. has no peremptory change of judge. A party seeking disqualification in a Superior Court civil case proceeds under the court’s own disqualification rule (Civil Rule 63-I), which is commonly understood to require a timely affidavit stating the facts of bias, with a certificate of counsel that it is made in good faith. The civil rules were not collected (dccourts.gov blocked automated access), so check the rule’s timing and form before filing.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 63-I (not collected)","v":false}],"speech":[{"t":"No state constitution; the First Amendment applies directly","d":"D.C. is a federal district, not a state, and has no state-constitution free-speech clause to read more broadly than the First Amendment. Local speech protection comes mainly from the Anti-SLAPP Act (see that section). Not checked against the collected texts.","c":"U.S. Const. amend. I; D.C. Code §§ 16-5501 to 16-5505","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Suing the District: written notice to the Mayor within 6 months","d":"An action against the District of Columbia for unliquidated damages to person or property cannot be maintained unless, within six months after the injury, the claimant or the claimant’s agent or lawyer gives the Mayor written notice of the approximate time, place, cause, and circumstances. A Metropolitan Police Department report made in regular course is sufficient notice. The rule does not apply to claims brought under § 2-1403.16 or § 1-615.54.","c":"D.C. Code § 12-309","v":true},{"t":"Short limitations periods: 1 year for defamation and intentional torts","d":"One year: libel, slander, assault, battery, mayhem, wounding, malicious prosecution, false arrest, false imprisonment, and statutory penalties. Three years: personal property, injury to real or personal property, simple contracts, and any claim with no other period. Twelve years: instruments under seal. Fifteen years: recovery of land. Contracts for the sale of goods follow UCC § 28:2-725 instead, and § 12-301 does not apply to suits by the District.","c":"D.C. Code § 12-301","v":true},{"t":"Superior Court follows the Federal Rules unless it modifies them","d":"By statute, the Superior Court conducts business under the Federal Rules of Civil and Criminal Procedure unless it adopts modifying rules, and a modifying rule needs the D.C. Court of Appeals’ approval. That is why the Superior Court Civil Rules track the federal numbering. Do not assume a local rule matches the federal one: read the D.C. text.","c":"D.C. Code § 11-946","v":true},{"t":"Interlocutory appeal by certification: apply within 10 days","d":"If a Superior Court judge states in writing that a civil ruling involves a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and that an immediate appeal may materially advance the case, the Court of Appeals may allow an appeal if you apply within ten days after the ruling is issued or entered. The application does not stay the trial court proceedings unless a judge orders a stay.","c":"D.C. Code § 11-721(d)","v":true}]}});
